# Build output. dist/ is the real one; dist-* are scratch builds used when more than one
# thing is being built at once, and must never be committed.
dist/
dist-*/
*.tsbuildinfo

# The hermetic browser, fetched into the repo by PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH. Large, and pinned
# by revision in renderer/vendor/VERSIONS.md rather than by being checked in.
.browsers/

node_modules/

# The CLIP vision encoder artist/resemblance.ts runs. 335MB, refetchable, and pinned by sha256 in
# that file rather than by being checked in — the same deal as .browsers/.
.models/

# Scratch render output and trajectories. goldens/ and examples/ are deliberately NOT ignored.
out/

# Render-metrics, env-model, judge and PNG caches (keyed by content hash; regenerable).
.cache/

# Corpus pixels. corpus/manifest.jsonl and corpus/readings/ ARE tracked — they are the evidence,
# they are small, and a provenance claim has to be checkable from a clone. The images are neither:
# every one is refetchable from the `image_url` on its manifest row and verifiable against the
# sha256 on the same row, which is what `corpus verify` does. Measured across all 41,511 of
# Cleveland's CC0 records the mean web derivative is 356KB, so tracking them would put 10.7GB in git
# at a 30,000-work corpus. Untracked at 50 works, while it was still free to do.
corpus/images/
corpus/failures.jsonl

# The candidate pool: a quarter of a million rows across three museums, ~110MB, regenerable by
# re-running `corpus metadata`. It is a fact about three public APIs rather than about this corpus.
# The argument for tracking the manifest was that the manifest is small; a file that is not small
# does not inherit that argument by having a similar name. What the pool would have justified — what
# was available, and why these works were chosen out of it — is in corpus/selection.json, which IS
# tracked.
corpus/pool.jsonl

# Env / secrets
.env
*.env
!.env.example

# OS
.DS_Store

# Worktrees live inside the repo so that one agent per worktree is easy to arrange, but a worktree is
# a second checkout of this same history — committing one would put the tree inside itself.
.claude/worktrees/

# The atlas and its viewer. Both are recomputed from `corpus/manifest.jsonl` in about three seconds
# by `corpus atlas`, and together they are 6.5MB of derived numbers. The manifest is the evidence;
# these are a reading of it.
corpus/atlas.json
corpus/atlas.html
corpus/atlas-clip.json
corpus/atlas-clip.html

# CLIP embeddings: 19,807 x 512 float32, one row per distinct image in sha256 order, plus the index
# that names the rows. 40.6MB of derived numbers that a local model reproduces from the pixels in
# twelve minutes with no API and no key. Derived, therefore not evidence, therefore not tracked —
# and a 40MB binary is the exact thing a repo of hashes and JSONL should not start carrying.
corpus/clip.f32
corpus/clip-index.json

# The second space, same rows in the same sha256 order: DINOv2-small's 384-d CLS token, 30MB,
# `node scripts/dino-embed.mjs`. Ignored for the same reasons and one more — it exists to disagree
# with the matrix above, and a tracked copy of it would be a second published space rather than a
# check on the first.
corpus/dino.f32
corpus/dino-index.json

# The text side of the same works: CLIP's TEXT tower over the catalogue prose, 20,000 x 512, one row
# per WORK in manifest order — not per image and not in sha256 order, so it does not row-align with
# the three matrices above and is joined by work id at read time. Two field sets, because
# `department` is an org chart rather than a description of the object and reading it as catalogue
# text is what made the catalogue look museum-bound. `npm run corpus -- text-embed`, no key.
corpus/text-clip-*.f32
corpus/text-clip-*.index.json

# The surface census: every image's open-field bands and the raw RenderMetrics of the 3,743 that
# have a confident ground, split by subject. 0.9MB, written by `corpus surface --all --save` and
# read by `corpus pack-gap`. Derived from `corpus/images/`, which is itself untracked — a tracked
# census would be a measurement of pixels nobody cloning this repo can see, and it would go stale
# silently the first time the ground detector changed.
corpus/surface.census.json

# Analytics exports: a flat-file view of the tracked manifest and the derived embeddings, for
# notebooks. `knn-k20.csv` alone is ~400k rows. Everything in here is reproduced by
# `npm run corpus -- export-analytics`, so it is a view and not a source.
corpus/analytics/

# Influence contact sheets: 97MB for seven positions, because each HTML inlines its own 1.8MB sheet
# as a data URI so the file can be opened or sent on its own with no server and no network. Derived
# in about two seconds each from `aesthetic/influences/*.resolved.json`, which IS tracked. The
# resolved sets are the evidence; these are a way of looking at them.
docs/demo/influences/

# Search contact sheets, same shape and same reason: 1.5MB of PNG per query, doubled by the HTML
# that inlines it. They cannot be rebuilt from a clone anyway — `corpus/images/` and `corpus/clip.f32`
# are both ignored — so tracking them would buy a fallback that only works where the fallback is not
# needed. The demo's tracked fallback is `docs/demo/screens/`, which is flat PNG at 1920x1080.
docs/demo/searches/

# The notebooks' Python. Pinned in notebooks/requirements.txt, which IS tracked; the venv itself is
# 200MB of wheels that pip rebuilds in a minute. `notebooks/out/` is scratch — notebooks may write
# there and nowhere else.
notebooks/.venv/
notebooks/out/

# The influence layer's Python. Same deal as notebooks/.venv: pinned in influence/requirements.txt,
# which IS tracked, and rebuilt by pip in about a minute. The worker itself
# (influence/descriptors.py) is tracked — it is the arithmetic, not the wheels.
influence/.venv/

# Python bytecode from the notebook loader.
__pycache__/
